    <h1>Peptide cold chain and logistics</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Lyophilized peptides are more forgiving than solutions, but transit heat, customs holds and humidity still matter. Here is the practical picture.</p>

    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/peptide-handling-laboratory-practice/">handling &amp; laboratory practice pillar</a> — the end-to-end chain from receipt to result.</p>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/peptide-shipping-customs-framework/">shipping &amp; customs pillar</a> — the end-to-end export framework.</p>
    <h2>Stability of lyophilized material in transit</h2>
    <p>A properly lyophilized cake with low residual moisture is stable at ambient temperature for days to weeks, depending on the peptide. The risk rises with heat exposure, humidity and time. Summer routes and customs holds are the two common failure points.</p>

    <h2>Packaging options</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Use case</th><th>Limitation</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ambient with desiccant</td><td>Short transit, stable peptides</td><td>Not for heat-sensitive compounds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Insulated box + phase-change packs</td><td>Summer routes, multi-day transit</td><td>Adds weight and cost</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cold-chain courier</td><td>Sensitive or high-value lots</td><td>Limited destinations, higher freight</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What to check on arrival</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Outer packaging intact; no signs of heat or water damage</li>
      <li>Phase-change packs still within their working range (if used)</li>
      <li>Vial seals and crimps intact</li>
      <li>Batch number matches the COA and invoice</li>
      <li>Cake appearance consistent with the certificate</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If a shipment arrives warm</h2>
    <p>Do not assume the material is ruined — lyophilized peptides tolerate short ambient exposure. Record the condition, photograph it, and ask the supplier for the stability data supporting ambient transit for that compound. If the lot is critical, test it before use.</p>

    <h2>Customs holds</h2>
    <p>A hold can extend transit by days or weeks. For sensitive compounds, plan for it: ship earlier, use insulated packaging, and keep the tracking active so you can intervene with the broker.</p>

    <div class="ruo">Research use only. This is logistics guidance, not a stability guarantee for any specific compound.</div>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>Cold chain is a chain only if every link is measured. A phase-change pack with no data logger is a hope with insulation.</p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>This is not legal or customs advice. Classification, permits and destination rules must be confirmed with a licensed broker and the relevant authority.</p>

